\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Coordinate reflections of the symmetric torus}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{On the periodic cubical torus with both directions of every
axis and with every weight equal to #{1}, each
\vocabk{site reflection}{fdip-007M} and each
\vocabk{link reflection}{fdip-007M} along one axis is a
\vocabk{reflection}{fdip-00PX}. The same formula on the
forward edges of one axis is not an automorphism of that
directed calculus. It does preserve the cellular chain
complex once a reversed edge changes sign.}

\proof{
\p{A coordinate reflection permutes the vertices and sends each
undirected edge of an axis to an undirected edge of the same
axis. Both directions are present, so the image of an oriented
edge is an oriented edge. Unit weights are constant, so the
permutation commutes with #{L} and with #{\covwedge}.}

\p{Now keep only the forward edges of the time axis, oriented
from #{t} to #{t+1}. The site reflection #{\theta(t)=-t} sends
the forward edge at #{t} to the edge from #{-t} to #{-t-1}.
That edge is the reverse of a forward edge, and the forward
calculus does not contain it. Thus #{\theta} is not an
automorphism of the forward calculus.}

\p{On the oriented time circle, send the forward generator
#{e_t} to the chain #{-e_{-t-1}}. The boundary
#{\partial e_t=(t+1)-(t)} is sent to
#{(-t-1)-(-t)}, which equals
#{\partial(-e_{-t-1})}. Spatial edges fixed by this
reflection contribute the same sign on both sides, so the
rule is a chain map of the oriented cellular complex.}
}
